{"agent":"architecture","findings":[{"id":"arch-001","file":"triggarr/clients/base.py","line":235,"title":"validate_connection now logs raw HTTPStatusError, bypassing the repo's established exception-sanitization pattern","category":"pattern-consistency","cwe":null,"severity":"medium","agent_confidence":85,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":null,"problem":"The diff changes the log value from `exc.response.status_code` to the raw `exc`. str(HTTPStatusError) includes the full request URL. The codebase already has an established way to handle this: `_sanitize_exc` in triggarr/search/engine.py:30-44 reduces HTTPStatusError to `HTTP {status_code}`. It is used at more than 20 call sites, including engine.py:366/420/487/619/881/1138, scheduler.py:228/362/387/407 and routes.py:968/1073. The same file follows the pattern inline at base.py:71-75 (`_request_with_retry`), and so does tracking.py:70-71. This hunk is the only place in the clients layer that logs a raw HTTPStatusError.","current_code":"logger.warning(
                    "{app}: Unexpected HTTP error: {exc}",
                    app=self._app_name,
                    exc=exc,
                )","fix_hint":"restore the status-code-only summary (f"HTTP {exc.response.status_code}") to match base.py:71-75 and _sanitize_exc; if a shared helper is wanted, move _sanitize_exc down into a clients/util module rather than importing search.engine from clients (that import would point the dependency the wrong way between layers)","why_it_matters":"The sanitization exists because str(exc) can carry the request URL (scheduler.py:401 documents stripping 'request.url credentials') past the loguru redacting sink. Logging the raw exception in one place breaks a guarantee the rest of the repo keeps on purpose, and future readers will copy the inconsistency.","silenced_marker_nearby":false,"needs_verification":null},{"id":"arch-002","file":"triggarr/clients/base.py","line":254,"title":"validate_connection now logs raw pydantic.ValidationError instead of the established error-count summary","category":"pattern-consistency","cwe":null,"severity":"medium","agent_confidence":82,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":null,"problem":"The diff replaces `count=exc.error_count()` with the raw `exc`. str(ValidationError) includes `input_value=` excerpts of the remote *arr response body. The established pattern is `_sanitize_exc` (engine.py:42-43, `validation error ({exc.error_count()} issues)`), which is used at more than 20 call sites in engine.py, scheduler.py and routes.py. The removed line followed that exact pattern.","current_code":"logger.warning(
                "{app}: Unexpected API response format: {exc}",
                app=self._app_name,
                exc=exc,
            )","fix_hint":"keep the error_count() summary consistent with _sanitize_exc; if more diagnostic detail is the goal, log only field locations (e['loc'] for e in exc.errors()), at debug level","why_it_matters":"Remote response content now goes into the warning log without passing through the repo's sanitization convention. This also reverses a choice the codebase made deliberately and documented in the _sanitize_exc docstring.","silenced_marker_nearby":false,"needs_verification":null}],"agent_notes":["The two changed hunks undo the sanitization convention documented in triggarr/search/engine.py:30-35 ('Avoids storing raw str(exc) which may contain internal paths, URLs, or API keys that bypass the loguru redacting sink'). The removed code matched that convention.","Raw `exc=exc` logging does exist elsewhere (config.py, state.py, changelog.py, update_check.py, routes.py:613/808), but only for local file-I/O or settings-validation errors. Every *arr HTTP/response error path uses the sanitized form, so the convention applies by error source, and these hunks are HTTP/response errors.","Layering: _sanitize_exc lives in triggarr/search/engine.py, and clients/base.py sits below search. Importing it into base.py would make clients depend on search, which is an upward dependency. Moving it into a lower-level module (e.g. a triggarr/clients helper) would let base.py, tracking.py and engine.py share one implementation. Right now there are 3 copies of the same HTTP-status summarizing logic (engine.py:36-37, base.py:71-75, tracking.py:70-71), which meets the rule of three. This is not emitted as a separate finding because the copies predate this diff.","No tests in tests/ assert on these log message strings, so the change has no test coupling.","The security lane should look at the same sites, since the change touches the CLAUDE.md 'No API keys in logs' convention. The API key travels in the X-Api-Key header, not the URL, so a key leak through str(HTTPStatusError) depends on the base_url carrying credentials. Pending: confirm whether user-configured URLs can contain userinfo or query-string keys."]}
